    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "we tried a fresh batch of ink and it's still doing the same thing. it's like the color is being actively prevented from sticking in those specific spots. even when we hold the press a bit longer or crank the heat slightly, those empty patches just stay empty. it feels almost like something is interfering with the transfer process right at the moment of contact. any ideas on what else might cause that kind of localized failure?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Localized failures point toward a non-thermal issue. Perhaps there's a build-up of dirt or oil in those specific spots on your polyester blend aiding in poor ink adhesion. Pay special attention to the condition of your substrate and consider thoroughly cleaning it. Another aspect to investigate is the evenness of your heat plate surface; imperfections or inconsistencies can affect heat transfer at a micro level. Also, check the printer's nozzle for any clogs, as this could lead to inconsistent ink flow. Lastly, the ink itself could be running thin or the pigment could be settling unevenly, causing insufficient transfer to some areas. Testing with different ink cartridges, including a new printhead, might provide further insights.",
      "source": "target",
